I want to thank Hostinger for sponsoring this part of the video, and now back to protein folding.

146
00:14:24,622 --> 00:14:34,148
As the AlphaFold 2 team searched for better algorithms, they turned to the transformer, that's the T in ChatGPT, and it relies on a concept called attention.

147
00:14:35,054 --> 00:14:38,884
In the sentence, the animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired.

148
00:14:39,406 --> 00:14:44,772
Attention recognizes that it refers to animal and not street based on the word tired.

149
00:14:45,710 --> 00:14:56,420
Attention adds context to any kind of sequential information by breaking it down into chunks, converting these into numerical representations or embeddings, and making connections between them.

150
00:14:56,846 --> 00:14:58,948
In this case, the word it and animal.

151
00:15:00,014 --> 00:15:04,772
3Blue1Brown has a great series of videos specifically about transformers and attention.

152
00:15:06,158 --> 00:15:13,444
Large language models use attention to predict the most appropriate word to add to a sentence, but AlphaFold also has sequential information.

153
00:15:13,742 --> 00:15:16,324
Not sentences, but amino acid sequences.

154
00:15:17,070 --> 00:15:22,880
And to analyze them, the AlphaFold team built their own version of the Transformer, called an Evoformer.

155
00:15:24,046 --> 00:15:32,484
The Evoformer contained two towers, evolutionary information in the biology tower, and pair representations in the geometry tower.

156
00:15:33,102 --> 00:15:37,828
Gone was Alpha Fold 1's deep neural network that started with one tower and predicted the other.

157
00:15:38,350 --> 00:15:42,084
Instead, AlphaFold II's Evoformer builds each tower separately.

158
00:15:42,478 --> 00:15:51,236
It starts with some initial guesses, evolutionary tables taken from known datasets as before, and the pair representations based on similar known proteins.

159
00:15:51,918 --> 00:15:59,044
And this time there's a bridge connecting the two towers that conveys newly found biological and geometry clues back and forth.

160
00:15:59,982 --> 00:16:10,500
In the biology tower, attention applied on a column identifies amino acid sequences that have been conserved, while along a row it finds amino acid mutations that have occurred together.

161
00:16:11,086 --> 00:16:20,644
Whenever the Evoformer finds too closely linked amino acids in the evolutionary table, it means they are important to structure, and it sends this information to the geometry tower.

162
00:16:20,942 --> 00:16:24,900
Here, attention is applied to help calculate distances between amino acids.

163
00:16:25,774 --> 00:16:32,896
There's also this thing called triangular attention that got introduced, which is essentially about letting triplets attend to each other.

164
00:16:33,262 --> 00:16:37,540
For each triplet of amino acids, AlphaFold applies the triangle inequality.

165
00:16:37,936 --> 00:16:40,996
The sum of two sides must be greater than the third.

166
00:16:41,486 --> 00:16:45,284
This constrains how far apart these three amino acids can be.

167
00:16:45,870 --> 00:16:48,996
This information is used to update the pair representation.

168
00:16:49,376 --> 00:16:53,380
And that helps the model produce a self-consistent picture of the structure.

169
00:16:53,806 --> 00:17:02,916
If the geometry tower finds it's impossible for two amino acids to be close to each other, then it tells the first tower to ignore their relationship in the evolutionary table.

170
00:17:03,470 --> 00:17:10,692
This exchange of information within the Evoformer goes on for 48 times until information within both towers is refined.

171
00:17:11,246 --> 00:17:18,336
The geometrical features learnt by this network are passed on to Alpha Fold 2's second main innovation, the structure module.

172
00:17:18,798 --> 00:17:24,416
For each amino acid, we pick three special atoms in the amino acid and say that those define a frame.

173
00:17:24,576 --> 00:17:34,820
And what the network does is it imagines that all the amino acids start out at the origin, and it has to predict the appropriate translation and rotation to move these frames to where they sit in the real structure.

174
00:17:35,086 --> 00:17:36,896
So that's essentially what the structure module does.

175
00:17:37,216 --> 00:17:41,216
But the thing that sets the structure module apart is what it doesn't do.

176
00:17:41,870 --> 00:17:50,656
Previously, people might have imagined that you would like to encode the fact that this is a chain, you know, and that you know, certain residues should sit next to each other.

177
00:17:50,830 --> 00:17:53,092
We don't really explicitly tell AlphaFold that.

178
00:17:53,216 --> 00:17:58,116
It's more like we give it a bag of amino acids and it's allowed to position each of them separately.

179
00:17:58,766 --> 00:18:05,348
And some people have thought that that helps it to not get stuck in terms of where things should be placed.

180
00:18:05,456 --> 00:18:08,644
It doesn't have to always be thinking about the constraint of these things forming a chain.

181
00:18:09,006 --> 00:18:10,884
That's something that emerges naturally later.

182
00:18:11,598 --> 00:18:17,936
That's why live AlphaFold folding videos can show it doing some weirdly non-physical stuff.

183
00:18:20,046 --> 00:18:24,484
The structure module outputs a 3D protein, but it still isn't ready.

184
00:18:24,736 --> 00:18:30,276
It's recycled at least three more times through the Evoformer to gain a deeper understanding of the protein.

185
00:18:30,734 --> 00:18:33,220
Only then the final prediction is made.

186
00:18:35,470 --> 00:18:40,452
In December 2020, DeepMind returned to a virtual CASP with AlphaFold 2.

187
00:18:41,006 --> 00:18:42,852
And this time, they did it.

188
00:18:43,886 --> 00:18:46,116
I'm going to read an email from John Malt.

189
00:18:46,606 --> 00:18:54,576
Your group has performed amazingly well in CASP 14, both relative to other groups and in absolute model accuracy.

190
00:18:54,816 --> 00:18:56,420
Congratulations on this work.

191
00:19:10,446 --> 00:19:17,600
For me, having worked on this problem so long, after many, many stops and starts, suddenly this is a solution.

192
00:19:17,930 --> 00:19:19,012
We solved the problem.

193
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:22,880
This gives you such excitement about the way science works.

194
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:31,972
Over six decades, all of the scientists working around the world on proteins painstakingly found about 150,000 protein structures.

195
00:19:32,654 --> 00:19:39,140
Then, in one fell swoop, Alpha Fold came in and unveiled over 200 million of them.

196
00:19:39,598 --> 00:19:43,012
Nearly all proteins known to exist in nature.

197
00:19:43,598 --> 00:19:49,920
In just a few months, Alpha Fold advanced the work of research labs worldwide by several decades.

198
00:19:50,990 --> 00:19:54,560
It has directly helped us develop a vaccine for malaria.

199
00:19:54,990 --> 00:20:01,732
It's made possible the breaking down of antibiotic-resistant enzymes, which make many life-saving drugs effective again.

200
00:20:02,318 --> 00:20:14,500
It's even helped us understand how protein mutations lead to various diseases, from schizophrenia to cancer, and biologists studying little-known and endangered species suddenly had access to proteins and their life mechanism.

201
00:20:15,598 --> 00:20:19,300
The AlphaFold 2 paper has been cited over 30,000 times.

202
00:20:19,726 --> 00:20:23,588
It has truly made a step-function leap in our understanding of life.

203
00:20:24,846 --> 00:20:30,372
John Jumper and Demis Hassabis were awarded one half of the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for this breakthrough.

204
00:20:30,894 --> 00:20:35,364
The other half went to David Baker, but not for predicting structures using Rosetta.

205
00:20:35,760 --> 00:20:39,760
Instead, it was for designing completely new proteins from scratch.

206
00:20:40,250 --> 00:20:42,852
It was really hard to make brand new proteins that would do things.

207
00:20:43,278 --> 00:20:44,930
And so that's kind of the problem that we solved.

208
00:20:45,582 --> 00:20:51,040
To do so, he uses the same kind of generative AI that makes art in programs like DALI.

209
00:20:51,598 --> 00:20:55,748
You can say, draw a picture of a kangaroo riding on a rabbit or something, and it will do that.

210
00:20:56,494 --> 00:20:58,212
And so it's exactly what we did with proteins.

211
00:20:58,560 --> 00:21:04,548
His technique, called RF diffusion, is trained by adding random noise to a known protein structure.

212
00:21:04,878 --> 00:21:07,364
And then the AI has to remove this noise.

213
00:21:07,822 --> 00:21:12,260
Once trained in this way, the AI can be asked to produce proteins for various functions.

214
00:21:12,622 --> 00:21:19,108
It's given a random noise input, and the AI figures out a brand new protein that does what you asked it to do.

215
00:21:20,366 --> 00:21:22,628
This work has huge implications.

216
00:21:22,926 --> 00:21:25,540
I mean, imagine you got bitten by a venomous snake.

217
00:21:26,094 --> 00:21:41,124
If you're lucky, you'll have access to antivenom, prepared by milking venom from the exact kind of snake, which is then injected into live animals, and the antibodies from that animal are extracted and refined and then given to you as an antivenom.

218
00:21:41,582 --> 00:21:46,500
The trouble is, often people have allergic reactions to these antibodies from other organisms.

219
00:21:46,862 --> 00:21:51,684
But your odds of survival can be a lot better with the latest synthetic proteins designed in Baker's lab.

220
00:21:52,174 --> 00:21:56,676
They've created human-compatible antibodies that can neutralize lethal snake venom.

221
00:21:57,102 --> 00:22:02,788
This antivenom could be manufactured in large quantities and easily transported to the places where it's needed.

222
00:22:03,950 --> 00:22:07,236
With these tiny molecular machines, the possibilities are endless.

223
00:22:07,918 --> 00:22:10,000
What are the applications you're most excited about?

224
00:22:10,382 --> 00:22:12,560
So I think vaccines are going to be really powerful.

225
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:18,080
We have a number of proteins that are in human clinical trials for cancer, and we're working on autoimmune disease now.

226
00:22:18,160 --> 00:22:26,320
We're really excited about problems like capturing greenhouse gases, so we're designing enzymes that can fix methane, break down plastic.

227
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:32,132
What makes this approach so effective is how fast they can create and iterate the proteins.

228
00:22:32,494 --> 00:22:37,860
It's really quite miraculous for anyone who's a conventional old school biochemist or protein scientist.

229
00:22:38,160 --> 00:22:47,760
We can now have designs on the computer, get the amino acid sequence of the designed proteins, and then in just a couple days, we can get the protein out.

230
00:22:47,920 --> 00:22:57,092
Yeah, we've given a name to this, which is cowboy biochemistry, because we just like we just you just gotta kind of go for it as fast as you can, and it turns out to work pretty well.

231
00:22:58,030 --> 00:23:04,132
What AI has done for proteins is just a hint of what it can do in other fields and on larger scales.

232
00:23:05,038 --> 00:23:17,668
In materials science, for example, DeepMind's GNOME program has found 2.2 million new crystals, including over 400,000 stable materials that could power future technologies, from superconductors to batteries.

233
00:23:18,510 --> 00:23:25,892
AI is creating transformative leaps in science by helping to solve some of the fundamental problems that have blocked human progress.

234
00:23:26,542 --> 00:23:31,520
If you think of the whole tree of knowledge, you know, there are certain problems where, you know, if there are root, no problems.

235
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:37,732
If you unlock them, if you discover a solution to them, it would unlock a whole new branch or avenue of discovery.

236
00:23:38,062 --> 00:23:44,160
And with this, AI is pushing forward the boundaries of human knowledge at a rate never seen before.

237
00:23:44,494 --> 00:23:46,020
You know, speedups of 2x are nice.

238
00:23:46,862 --> 00:23:47,268
They're great.

239
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:48,068
We love them.

240
00:23:49,038 --> 00:23:52,996
Speedups of 100,000 times change what you do.

241
00:23:53,230 --> 00:24:00,676
You do fundamentally different stuff, and you start to rebuild your science around the things that got easy.

242
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:03,332
And that's what I'm excited about.

243
00:24:03,758 --> 00:24:07,940
These discoveries represent real step-function changes in science.

244
00:24:08,366 --> 00:24:14,404
Even if AI doesn't advance beyond where it is today, we will be reaping the benefits of these breakthroughs for decades.

245
00:24:14,958 --> 00:24:28,676
And assuming AI does continue to develop, well, it will open up opportunities that were previously thought impossible, whether that's curing all diseases, creating novel materials, or restoring the environment to a pristine state.

246
00:24:29,422 --> 00:24:35,748
This sounds like an amazing future as long as the AI doesn't take over and destroy us all first.

